Z||000000||MooCow||07-25-2000||11:12 PM||||How do I fix this problem. Everytime I put it back on the next day it falls off.  I dont even know if it is telling the right temperature or not.  I do know that it moves but waht way should I face the line when putting it back up and can I glue it on??<P>                                   Thanks Ken||||reg||11
Z||000001||tgowens||07-26-2000||12:01 AM||||MooCow,<P>I noticed that no one had answered you here -- I am not meaning to be critical but, I, for one, have no clue what you are saying....I have an '87GT and my temp gauge is a part of the main instrument cluster and behind is only removed by disassembling the main instrument cluster. In other words, there is no way it can fall off......<P>It would help all of us here a little more if we knew what year and model Fiero you are referring to as well.......<P>So, I'm sure someone here has had a similar problem and quite possibly could help if you would just provide a little more information for us, ok?<P>Thanks...........<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 07-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||07-26-2000||12:06 AM||||hmmmmmm.......im guessing the NEEDLE is falling off.  Just put it on with the needle facing 0 and glue it on.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||07-26-2000||12:40 AM||||you need to first let the car sit overnight so it is cool, Then use an epoxy and epoxy the needle pointing 1 needle mark width below 100 (low end of the scale) I used Dupont 5-minute epoxy on mine. Don't use super glue unless you get the gel kind. If the glue is too thin it could run down the shaft and glue the gage. This is a common problem with the temp and sometimes the gas gage. The easist way to see if the needle is loose is to drive until the needle is at a known spot then turn sharply left or right. If needle shifts in that direction and stays there, it usually means the needle is loose on the shaft. The temp gage seems to be more prone if the needle pegs on startup for a second. The pegging needle is an intermitant problem that can be fixed by adding a ground wire.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||07-27-2000||09:32 AM||||Sorry for the confusion it was the needle that kept falling off.  I will go find some epoxy glue to use on it.  Thanks for helping me out.<P>                                 Thanks Ken||||reg||10
Z||000005||batboy||07-27-2000||11:57 AM||||I use this stuff call Shoe Goo sold in the sporting goods section of many department store (Wal-Mart, etc.). I started using it years ago to patch and repair my caving boots (caving is super rough on boots). Over time, I discovered it works on lots of thing. I've glued emblems back onto the outside of cars, I glued a plastic fan blade back together and the fan has been working ok for years, and the list goes on and on. Great stuff, maybe not as strong as epoxy for some specialized things, but certainly lots cheaper and easy to use.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaveL||07-27-2000||03:45 PM||||Had the same problem with the needle on my 87 SE. On mine there are two colored dots on the platform the needle is mounted on. Just mount the needle to these two dots, if they aren't there it might be that I just needed to clean my sunglasses. Just got lucky and mounted it close. Actually I can still see them. Gotta go, I've got an appointment with my optometrist...||||reg||1
